/* ==========================================================================
   EXAM 3 ADDITIONS — Modules 1, 2 and 3
   ==========================================================================
   Each tests a trap or phrasing of hers that the bank did not yet ask her
   way: her True/False polls, the other version of Quiz 1, IV Infusions
   Practice 3 parts c to i, and two select-all items in the form of her Exam 2
   review item 6. Every numeric key is recomputed at full precision; `audit`
   names the item of hers each one is built on.
   ========================================================================== */
const Q_NEW_A = [
{"id": "x3-a-tf-rate", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 1, "lecture": "L01", "topic": "orders", "sub": "first", "concept": "first-order-rate-depends", "skill": "tell", "source": "both", "dupOf": "m1-ord-c04", "stem": "The rate of a first-order process is independent of the concentration of drug present.", "options": [{"t": "True", "why": "Picking True reads \"independent\" as a first-order property. In a first-order process the rate is k times the concentration, dC/dt = −kC, so the rate is fastest where there is most drug and slows as the drug is eliminated. A rate independent of concentration is zero order, dC/dt = −k."}, {"t": "False", "correct": true, "why": "The first-order rate depends on the concentration present: dC/dt = −kC. On the curve the slope is steepest at the start, where there is most drug, and flattens as the drug is eliminated. What is independent of concentration in first order is the half-life, {{frac:0.693|k}}."}], "teach": [{"h": "Rate and half-life in the two orders", "list": ["First order: the rate depends on the concentration, {{frac:dC|dt}} = −kC; the half-life does not, t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}.", "Zero order: the rate is independent of the concentration, {{frac:dC|dt}} = −k; the half-life depends on it, t½ = {{frac:C0|2k}}.", "Read which quantity the statement names, the rate or the half-life, before answering."]}], "quote": "If we're thinking about the rate being the slope, higher, steeper slope up here where we've got more drug in the body ... so the rate depends on the concentration that's there", "audit": "PollEv on 19 Aug (transcript 08-19), printed on annotated Introduction deck page 25: \"The rate of a first-order process is independent of the concentration of drug present. True False\". Class \"fifty-fifty\"; keyed False from her debrief.", "cite": "Introduction.pdf, poll slide following slide 21"},
{"id": "x3-a-tf-half", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 1, "lecture": "L01", "topic": "orders", "sub": "half", "concept": "first-order-halflife", "skill": "tell", "source": "both", "dupOf": "m1-ord-c03", "stem": "The half-life of a first-order process is independent of the concentration of drug present.", "options": [{"t": "True", "correct": true, "why": "First-order half-life is t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}: a constant divided by a constant. No concentration appears in it, so the time to fall from 100 to 50 mg/L is the same as from 10 to 5 mg/L, whatever dose was given."}, {"t": "False", "why": "Picking False reads \"independent\" as \"dependent\", or carries the zero-order half-life, t½ = {{frac:C0|2k}}, into first order. Only in zero order does the starting concentration set the half-life. In first order the rate changes with concentration and the half-life does not."}], "teach": [{"h": "Rate and half-life in the two orders", "list": ["First order: the rate depends on the concentration, {{frac:dC|dt}} = −kC; the half-life does not, t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}.", "Zero order: the rate is independent of the concentration, {{frac:dC|dt}} = −k; the half-life depends on it, t½ = {{frac:C0|2k}}.", "t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} is not on the equation sheet."]}], "quote": "So no matter how much drug you have for a first-order process, the half-life will be the half-life, 0.693 over the rate constant K", "audit": "PollEv opening the 24 Aug lecture (annotated ivb deck page 1: \"The half-life of a first-order process is independent of the concentration of drug present. True False\"). \"Most of you guys said true\"; keyed True (transcript 08-24).", "cite": "Introduction.pdf slide 19; 2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, opening PollEv slide"},
{"id": "x3-a-q1-zero", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 1, "lecture": "L01", "topic": "orders", "sub": "zero", "concept": "zero-order-defn", "skill": "tell", "source": "both", "multi": true, "dupOf": "cq1-1", "stem": "Which of the following is true about a process in which the rate is independent of the concentration? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "The half-life depends on the concentration of the drug present.", "correct": true, "why": "A rate independent of concentration is zero order, and the zero-order half-life is t½ = {{frac:C0|2k}}: the starting concentration sits in it, so a higher starting level takes longer to halve."}, {"t": "The half-life is independent of concentration.", "why": "Picking this carries the first-order half-life across. t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} holds only when the rate depends on concentration. Options a and b contradict each other, so a select-all can never key both."}, {"t": "The rate equation is dC/dt = −kC.", "why": "dC/dt = −kC makes the rate k times the concentration, so the rate depends on concentration: first order, the opposite of the process in the stem."}, {"t": "The rate equation is dC/dt = −k.", "correct": true, "why": "dC/dt = −k has no concentration term, so the same amount is lost per unit time whatever is present. That is the zero-order rate law the stem describes."}], "teach": [{"h": "The two versions of this item", "list": ["Rate independent of concentration: zero order, {{frac:dC|dt}} = −k, and t½ = {{frac:C0|2k}} depends on C0.", "Rate depends on concentration: first order, {{frac:dC|dt}} = −kC, and t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} is constant.", "Each version keys one half-life option and one rate equation; the two half-life options never go together."]}], "quote": "Our stem says independent of concentration, so you should have picked for this one, A and D.", "audit": "The other printed version of Quiz 1 Q1 (STYLE.md, Quiz 1; versions handed out with the stems flipped). Options a-c as read aloud on 26 Aug; d is not read out and is dC/dt = -k by the option list of the version in cq1-1. Key \"A and D\" (transcript 08-26).", "cite": "Quiz 1 (26 Aug), other version, question 1; her spoken key, transcript 08-26; Introduction.pdf slides 17 and 19"},
{"id": "x3-a-q2-first", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 1, "lecture": "L01", "topic": "orders", "sub": "first", "concept": "first-order-rate-depends", "skill": "tell", "source": "both", "dupOf": "cq1-2", "stem": "Which of the following is true about a first-order process?", "options": [{"t": "The elimination rate is at its highest when the amount of drug in the body is at its highest.", "correct": true, "why": "First order means the rate is k times the amount present, dC/dt = −kC, so the most drug gives the fastest loss: the rate is highest at the top of the curve and slows as the amount falls."}, {"t": "The elimination rate is at its highest when the amount of drug in the body is at its lowest.", "why": "Picking this reverses the proportion. In first order the rate falls as the amount falls, so the lowest amount gives the slowest loss, never the fastest."}, {"t": "The elimination rate is independent of the amount of drug in the body.", "why": "A rate independent of the amount is zero order, dC/dt = −k. It is the key to the other version of this item, which asked about a zero-order process."}, {"t": "The elimination rate increases as a function of time.", "why": "Time enters only through the amount already lost. In first order the amount falls with time, so the rate falls with time; no elimination process in this course speeds up as time passes."}], "teach": [{"h": "First order", "list": ["{{frac:dC|dt}} = −kC: the rate is the rate constant times what is present.", "Most drug, fastest loss: the curve is steepest at the start and flattens.", "The fraction lost per hour, k, and the half-life stay fixed while the rate falls."]}], "quote": "A, right? The rate is highest at the top of the curve when the concentration is highest, OK.", "audit": "The other printed version of Quiz 1 Q2 (first-order stem); options as read aloud on 26 Aug; key A (transcript 08-26).", "cite": "Quiz 1 (26 Aug), other version, question 2; her spoken key, transcript 08-26; Introduction.pdf slide 18"},
{"id": "x3-a-pct", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 1, "lecture": "L01", "topic": "orders", "sub": "half", "concept": "halflife-counting", "skill": "krate", "source": "transcript", "stem": "The half-life of a drug in the body is 4 hours, and the drug is eliminated by a first-order process. What percent of the dose remains in the body 8 hours after the dose is given?", "units": "%", "answer": 25, "tol": 0.5, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "8 hr ÷ 4 hr = 2 half-lives", "why": "Each half-life removes half of what is present, so the elapsed time is counted in half-lives first. The dose does not enter, because a first-order process removes the same fraction from any starting amount."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Remaining = 100 × (0.5)(0.5) = 25 per cent of the dose", "why": "After one half-life half remains; after the second, half of that half. The question asks what remains, not what has been eliminated, which is the other 75 per cent."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Check: 100 × e^(−(0.17325)(8)) = 100 × e^(−1.386) = 25.0", "why": "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.17325 hr⁻¹, and e^(−kt) is the fraction remaining at time t. It gives the same 25 per cent as the half-life count."}, {"k": "round", "t": "25%", "why": "Two whole half-lives leave exactly one quarter, so nothing is rounded."}], "setup": {"eq": "amount-exp", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "\"first-order process\" with a half-life, and \"what percent of the dose remains\" at 8 hours, so the amount decline D = D0 e^(−kt) written as a percentage. Eight hours is two half-lives, so one half times one half remains."}, "asks": "pct", "givens": [["t½", "4 hours", "eight hours holds two of them"], ["t", "8 hours", "the elapsed time"], ["D0", "the dose", "taken as 100 per cent at time zero"]], "check": {"t": "Two half-lives halve the dose twice: 100 to 50 to 25 per cent remaining, while 75 per cent has been eliminated.", "lo": 20, "hi": 30}, "teach": [{"h": "Remaining against eliminated", "list": ["Remaining after 1, 2, 3 half-lives: 50%, 25%, 12.5%. Eliminated: 50%, 75%, 87.5%.", "Read which one the stem asks for before answering.", "The percentages hold for any dose, because the half-life of a first-order process does not depend on the dose."]}], "quote": "So I, so my question to you was how much was eliminated, and you said 75%. Right, so 25% remains.", "audit": "Her 19 Aug example (transcript 08-19): \"half-life of this drug in the body is gonna be 4 hours ... 2 Half-Lives is 8 hours\". She asked the percent eliminated (75%); this item asks the percent remaining. Recomputed 100 e^(-0.17325*8) = 25.007.", "cite": "Introduction.pdf slide 19"},
{"id": "x3-a-kpos", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 1, "lecture": "L01", "topic": "orders", "sub": "first", "concept": "first-order-properties", "skill": "recall", "source": "transcript", "stem": "Which of the following is true about the rate constant k of a zero-order or a first-order process?", "options": [{"t": "It is never a negative number", "correct": true, "why": "The concentration falls, so the slope of the line is negative, and k is the slope with its sign reversed. The minus sign is written into the equations, C = C0 − kt and C = C0e^(−kt), so k itself is always reported as a positive number."}, {"t": "It is negative for a falling concentration", "why": "Picking this puts the decline into k twice. The fall is already the minus sign in the equation; a negative k there would make the concentration rise. A negative k from two points means they were subtracted in the wrong order."}, {"t": "It has units of reciprocal time in both orders", "why": "Only the first-order constant is reciprocal time, such as hr⁻¹. A zero-order k is itself a rate, in concentration or amount per unit time, such as mg/mL/hr."}, {"t": "It changes with the starting concentration in first order", "why": "The first-order k is a fixed fraction per unit time and holds no concentration, which is why the first-order half-life, {{frac:0.693|k}}, is constant. The starting concentration enters the zero-order half-life, {{frac:C0|2k}}, not k."}], "teach": [{"h": "Signs and units of k", "list": ["Both orders report k as a positive number; the minus sign lives in the equations.", "First order: k = {{frac:ln(C1 ÷ C2)|t2 − t1}}, the earlier, larger concentration on top; units of reciprocal time.", "Zero order: k = {{frac:C1 − C2|t2 − t1}}, earlier minus later; units of concentration or amount per time."]}], "quote": "our rate constant K, whether we're talking 1st order or zero order, our rate constant is never going to be a negative. It's never gonna be a negative number", "audit": "Spoken 19 Aug (transcript 08-19) while deriving C = C0 - kt from the negative slope; the annotated Introduction deck page 20 carries the same note in the student's hand.", "cite": "Introduction.pdf slides 17 and 18"},
{"id": "x3-a-tf-cl", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 2, "lecture": "L02", "topic": "bolus1", "sub": "cl", "concept": "cl-constant", "skill": "apply", "source": "transcript", "dupOf": "m2-cl-3", "stem": "Clearance increases as concentration increases.", "options": [{"t": "True", "why": "Picking True confuses clearance with the rate of elimination. The amount removed per hour, Cl × Cp, does rise with the concentration, but clearance itself, the volume of plasma cleared per hour, is k × VD, a constant times a constant."}, {"t": "False", "correct": true, "why": "Clearance is k × VD. Both are constants for a first-order drug in a given patient, so clearance does not change when the concentration changes. What rises with concentration is the amount eliminated per hour, Cl × Cp, in mg/hr."}], "teach": [{"h": "Clearance", "list": ["Clearance (Cl) is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time, in L/hr.", "Cl = k × VD; it is not on the equation sheet.", "The rate of elimination, in mg/hr, is Cl × Cp: it rises and falls with the concentration while Cl stays fixed."]}], "quote": "We like clearance because it is a constant, OK? So, changing the concentration does not change the clearance.", "audit": "PollEv, 24 Aug (transcript 08-24): \"Clearance increases as concentration increases. True or false ... Most of you guys said false ... that is what I was looking for\". The stem is her spoken wording.", "cite": "2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slide \"Clearance\""},
{"id": "x3-a-ivb-t", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 2, "lecture": "L02", "topic": "bolus1", "sub": "calc", "concept": "thalf-two-points", "skill": "krate", "source": "both", "stem": "A new drug was administered as a single IV dose of 200 mg to an 80-kg adult male. After 6 hours, the plasma drug concentration was 15 mg/L of plasma. Assuming that the apparent VD is 10% of body weight, what is the half-life of this drug in this patient?", "units": "hr", "answer": 8.14, "tol": 0.12, "steps": [{"k": "unit", "t": "VD = (0.10)(80 kg) = 8 kg of body weight = 8 L", "why": "A volume given as a percent of body weight is read with 1 kg of body weight taken as 1 L, so 10% of 80 kg is 8 L."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "DB at 6 hr = VD × Cp = (8 L)(15 mg/L) = 120 mg", "why": "The dose is an amount and the 6-hour value is a concentration, so one of them is converted before the two are compared. Litres times mg/L leaves mg."}, {"k": "setup", "t": "k = {{frac:ln(D0 ÷ DB)|t}} = {{frac:ln(200 mg ÷ 120 mg)|6 hr}}", "why": "The dose is the amount at time zero and 120 mg is the amount at 6 hours, so the first-order line through the two amounts gives k. The milligrams cancel inside the logarithm."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "k = {{frac:0.5108|6 hr}} = 0.08514 hr⁻¹", "why": "A pure number divided by hours leaves reciprocal hours, the unit of a first-order rate constant."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "t½ = {{frac:0.693|0.08514 hr⁻¹}} = 8.14 hr", "why": "t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}, the relation she expects known without the equation sheet. A pure number over reciprocal hours leaves hours."}, {"k": "round", "t": "t½ = 8.14 hr", "why": "With k rounded to 0.085 hr⁻¹ the same division gives 8.15 hr, her figure; both are accepted."}], "setup": {"eq": "thalf-first", "pre": ["cp-db-vd", "amount-exp"], "why": "\"single IV dose\" with one concentration at 6 hours and VD as a percent of body weight; the half-life is asked, so t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}. k comes first, from the dose and the amount at 6 hours on D = D0 e^(−kt), and that amount from Cp = {{frac:DB|VD}}."}, "asks": "thalf", "givens": [["D0", "200 mg", "the amount at time zero"], ["weight", "80-kg", "with 10% gives VD"], ["Cp at 6 hr", "15 mg/L", "times VD gives the amount at 6 hours"], ["VD", "10% of body weight", "8 L, taking 1 kg as 1 L"]], "check": {"t": "120 mg of the 200 mg dose is still in the body at 6 hours, more than half, so 6 hours is less than one half-life and t½ is over 6 hr: 8.14 hr fits.", "lo": 6, "hi": 12}, "teach": [{"h": "Amount against concentration", "list": ["The dose is an amount (mg); a plasma level is a concentration (mg/L). A rate constant needs two amounts or two concentrations.", "VD converts one into the other: amount = VD × Cp.", "Here VD = 8 L, so 15 mg/L at 6 hours is 120 mg in the body, and k = {{frac:ln(200 ÷ 120)|6 hr}}."]}], "quote": "OK, and then the Half-Life, what's the Half-Life? 8. 8.15 hours.", "audit": "The second question printed on the deck slide \"Practice\" (annotated ivb page 7); m2-n-deck12 asks the first. Her answer 8.15 hr (transcript 08-24) uses k = 0.085; unrounded k = 0.0851376 gives 8.140 hr (0.693/k) or 8.141 hr (ln 2/k). Tolerance 0.12 accepts both.", "cite": "2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slide \"Practice\" (one-compartment section)"},
{"id": "x3-a-ivb-c", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 2, "lecture": "L02", "topic": "bolus1", "sub": "calc", "concept": "cp-at-time", "skill": "conctime", "source": "transcript", "dupOf": "m2-n-deck12", "stem": "A new drug was administered as a single IV dose of 200 mg to an 80-kg adult male. After 6 hours, the plasma drug concentration was 15 mg/L of plasma. The apparent VD is 10% of body weight. What is the plasma concentration of the drug 12 hours after the dose was administered? (Part a: the amount of drug in the body at 12 hours is 72 mg.)", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 9, "tol": 0.2, "steps": [{"k": "unit", "t": "VD = (0.10)(80 kg) = 8 L", "why": "10% of body weight, with 1 kg of body weight taken as 1 L."}, {"k": "setup", "t": "Cp = {{frac:DB|VD}}", "why": "The volume of distribution relates the amount in the body to the plasma concentration at any time, not only at time zero, so the amount at 12 hours divided by VD is the concentration at 12 hours."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = {{frac:72 mg|8 L}} = 9 mg/L", "why": "mg over L leaves mg/L. The 72 mg is an amount; reporting it as the answer would give an amount where a concentration is asked."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 9 mg/L", "why": "Her answer is 9 mg/L. The long route gives the same: C0 = {{frac:200 mg|8 L}} = 25 mg/L, and 25 × e^(−(0.08514)(12)) = 9.0 mg/L."}], "setup": {"eq": "cp-db-vd", "pre": [], "why": "The \"plasma concentration\" is asked from an amount in the body and a VD given as a percent of body weight, so Cp = {{frac:DB|VD}} directly. VD is 10% of 80 kg, 8 L."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["DB at 12 hr", "72 mg", "the amount, divided by VD"], ["VD", "10% of body weight", "8 L for the 80-kg patient"], ["weight", "80-kg", "with 10% gives VD"], ["D0", "200 mg", "not needed once the 72 mg is known"]], "check": {"t": "The level was 15 mg/L at 6 hours and keeps falling, so at 12 hours it must be below 15 mg/L: 72 mg spread through 8 L is 9 mg/L.", "lo": 0, "hi": 15}, "teach": [{"h": "Apples to apples", "list": ["An amount (mg) and a concentration (mg/L) are different quantities; VD is the go-between: Cp = {{frac:DB|VD}}.", "Read whether the stem asks for the amount in the body or the concentration in the plasma.", "The same 12 hours gives 72 mg in the body and 9 mg/L in the plasma."]}], "quote": "So you take that 72, you divide it by the volume of distribute that 72 ... which we said was 8 L. And you should get 9 mg per liter as the concentration 12 hours after that dose was administered.", "audit": "Her spoken add-on to the slide question (transcript 08-24): \"Then, impromptu, I added, what's the concentration? That's not on the slide.\" Key 9 mg/L = 72/8. Recomputed: D12 = 200 e^(-0.0851376*12) = 72.00 mg; 72/8 = 9.000 mg/L.", "cite": "2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slide \"Practice\" (one-compartment section)"},
{"id": "x3-a-dose2", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 2, "lecture": "L02", "topic": "bolus1", "sub": "vd", "concept": "c0-proportional-to-dose", "skill": "apply", "source": "both", "multi": true, "dupOf": "ws2-4-1f", "stem": "Which of the following would you expect with a 2-fold increase in the IV bolus dose of a drug that follows first-order elimination? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Increased initial plasma concentration", "correct": true, "why": "C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}} with VD fixed, so twice the dose gives twice C0. In her practice key 1200 mg in 24.8 L gives 48.387 mg/L, twice the 24.19 mg/L from 600 mg."}, {"t": "Increased AUC", "correct": true, "why": "Clearance relates the dose to the area under the curve, Cl = {{frac:D0|AUC}}, so AUC = {{frac:D0|Cl}}. Clearance does not change with the dose, so twice the dose gives twice the AUC."}, {"t": "Increased elimination half-life", "why": "Picking this reads a larger amount as taking longer to halve. t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} has no dose in it; the half-life is constant for a first-order process, so 600 mg and 1200 mg halve in the same time."}, {"t": "Increased clearance", "why": "Picking this confuses clearance with the rate of elimination. The mg removed per hour, Cl × Cp, doubles; the volume cleared per hour, Cl = k × VD, does not change with the dose."}, {"t": "Increased apparent volume of distribution", "why": "VD is a proportionality constant of the drug in the patient. More drug gives a higher concentration in the same apparent volume; the volume does not change with the dose."}], "teach": [{"h": "What a dose change moves, first order", "list": ["In proportion to the dose: C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}}, every later concentration, the amount in the body, and AUC = {{frac:D0|Cl}}.", "Unchanged by the dose: k, t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}}, VD and Cl = k × VD.", "The mg removed per hour, Cl × Cp, doubles with the dose; clearance in L/hr does not."]}], "quote": "So, remember the other pieces here for first-order processes, our clearance, our half-life, and our volume of distribution. These things are going to be constant.", "audit": "Stem in the form of her Exam 2 review item 6 (\"Which of the following would you expect with a 2-fold increase in oral dose? (Select all that apply)\"), applied to her 2 Sep poll (infusion deck page 1) and IV Bolus Practice 4 parts d-f. AUC = D0/Cl from the deck slide \"Clearance\" (ClT = kVD = D0/AUC).", "cite": "2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slide \"Clearance\"; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, opening PollEv slide; IV-Bolus-Practice-4---Solutions.pdf, problem 1 parts d to f"},
{"id": "x3-a-2cpt-c4", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 2, "lecture": "L03", "topic": "bolus2", "sub": "calc", "concept": "biexp-conc-at-t", "skill": "conctime", "source": "both", "stem": "A 250-mg IV bolus dose of a drug is described by A = 8.45 mg/L, B = 5.32 mg/L, α = 2.18 hr⁻¹ and β = 0.114 hr⁻¹. What is the concentration of drug in the plasma 4 hours after administration of the dose?", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 3.37, "tol": 0.07, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "Cp = Ae^(−αt) + Be^(−βt)", "why": "A two-compartment concentration is the sum of two exponential terms, each intercept with its own rate constant: A with α, B with β."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "A term = (8.45 mg/L)e^(−(2.18)(4)) = (8.45 mg/L)e^(−8.72) = 0.0014 mg/L", "why": "hr⁻¹ times hr leaves a pure number. The distribution term has all but vanished by 4 hours because α is large."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "B term = (5.32 mg/L)e^(−(0.114)(4)) = (5.32 mg/L)(0.6338) = 3.372 mg/L", "why": "The elimination term carries the answer at 4 hours; e^(−0.456) is the fraction of B left."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = 0.0014 + 3.372 = 3.373 mg/L", "why": "Both terms are concentrations in mg/L, so they add."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 3.37 mg/L", "why": "Her answer for this sheet is 3.4 mg/L, the same value to one decimal place; both are accepted."}], "setup": {"eq": "biexp", "pre": [], "why": "A, B, α and β for an \"IV bolus dose\" name the two-compartment block, and the concentration \"4 hours after\" is asked, so Cp = A e^(−αt) + B e^(−βt) evaluated at t = 4 hr. No hinge."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["A", "8.45 mg/L", "pairs with α"], ["B", "5.32 mg/L", "pairs with β"], ["α", "2.18 hr⁻¹", "distribution: its term is negligible by 4 hours"], ["β", "0.114 hr⁻¹", "elimination: carries the answer"], ["D0", "250-mg", "not used for a concentration"]], "check": {"t": "By 4 hours the α term has all but vanished, and the B term keeps a little under two thirds of 5.32 mg/L: 3.37 mg/L, below B and above zero.", "lo": 0, "hi": 5.32}, "teach": [{"h": "Two-compartment concentration at a time", "list": ["Put the time into both exponentials, multiply each by its own intercept, and add.", "Several α half-lives out, the first term is effectively zero and the curve is the β line alone.", "She gives A, B, α and β and does not name the model."]}], "quote": "Concentration, 4 hours after administration of the dose, 3.4 mg per liter.", "audit": "Parameters as in m2-n-koverall (her in-class two-compartment sheet; the annotated ivb deck page 27 records 3.37 mg/L). Her spoken answer 3.4 mg/L (transcript 08-26). Recomputed 8.45e^(-8.72) + 5.32e^(-0.456) = 0.00138 + 3.37189 = 3.3733.", "cite": "2IVBolusAdministration.pdf, slide \"Concentration of Drug in the Central Compartment\"; in-class two-compartment practice sheet problem 1, answers read aloud in Basic Pharmacokinetics - 08-26 Lecture.txt"},
{"id": "x3-a-rate2", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "css", "concept": "rate-vs-time-to-css", "skill": "apply", "source": "both", "multi": true, "dupOf": "cq2-6", "stem": "Which of the following would you expect with a 2-fold increase in the rate of a continuous IV infusion? Select all that apply.", "options": [{"t": "Increased steady-state concentration", "correct": true, "why": "Css = {{frac:R|Cl}} and clearance is constant for the patient, so doubling R doubles Css. In her review, 50 mg/hr gave about 12 mg/L and 100 mg/hr about 24 mg/L."}, {"t": "Increased plasma level at 2 hours", "correct": true, "why": "During the infusion Cp = ({{frac:R|Cl}})(1 − e^(−kt)). The bracket holds only k and t, so doubling R doubles the level at every time, including 2 hours into the infusion."}, {"t": "Increased time needed to reach steady state", "why": "Picking this reads a higher plateau as a longer climb. The fraction of steady state reached, 1 − e^(−kt), has no R in it, so either rate takes 3 to 5 half-lives."}, {"t": "Decreased time needed to reach steady state", "why": "Picking this reads a faster input as a faster arrival. Changing the rate changes only the concentration; the time to steady state stays 3 to 5 half-lives, set by k."}, {"t": "Increased elimination half-life", "why": "The half-life is {{frac:0.693|k}}, and k = {{frac:Cl|VD}} belongs to the drug in the patient. The infusion rate is a dosing choice and changes neither Cl nor VD."}], "teach": [{"h": "Two halves of the infusion equation", "list": ["Cp = {{frac:R|Cl}}(1 − e^(−kt)).", "{{frac:R|Cl}} is Css, the height of the plateau: it moves with R.", "1 − e^(−kt) is the fraction of Css reached: it holds only k and t, so the time to steady state, 3 to 5 half-lives, does not move with R.", "A loading dose, not a faster rate, brings the level to Css at once."]}], "quote": "Changing the rate does not change the time that it takes to get to steady state. Changing the rate only changes the steady-state concentration.", "audit": "Her Exam 2 review select-all form applied to Quiz 2 Q6, the 2 Sep lecture (\"I've said it 5 times ... I'm gonna ask it of you 10 times\") and the 19 Oct follow-ups (12 then 24 mg/L at 50 then 100 mg/hr). 2-hour level at 100 mg/hr: 100/4.158 x 0.3700 = 8.90 mg/L, twice 4.45.", "cite": "Canvas Quiz 2 (Modules 2–3), question 6; Final-Exam-Review-10.19.pdf slide 25; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration at Steady-State\""},
{"id": "x3-a-half-ss", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "pre", "concept": "cp-before-steady-state", "skill": "conctime", "source": "transcript", "stem": "A drug with a half-life of 4 hours is infused at a constant rate that gives a steady-state concentration of 20 mg/L. No loading dose is given. What is the plasma concentration 4 hours after the infusion is started?", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 10, "tol": 0.2, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "4 hr ÷ 4 hr = 1 half-life of infusion", "why": "The fraction of steady state reached depends only on how many half-lives have passed, so the time is counted in half-lives first."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Fraction of Css after one half-life = 0.5", "why": "One half-life closes half of the gap between zero and the plateau. For an infusion, one half-life is the time to reach half of the steady-state concentration."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = (0.5)(20 mg/L) = 10 mg/L", "why": "The fraction has no units, so the answer keeps the mg/L of the plateau."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Long route: Cp = (20 mg/L)(1 − e^(−0.693)) = 10 mg/L", "why": "kt = ({{frac:0.693|4 hr}})(4 hr) = 0.693, so the bracket is one half: the same answer by the equation."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 10 mg/L", "why": "Exactly one half-life, so nothing is rounded."}], "setup": {"eq": "cp-infusing", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "\"infused at a constant rate\" with no loading dose, and the level \"4 hours after the infusion is started\", so Cp = Css(1 − e^(−kt)). Four hours is one half-life, so the bracket is one half."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["Css", "20 mg/L", "the plateau the infusion approaches"], ["t½", "4 hours", "the elapsed 4 hours is one half-life"], ["t", "4 hours", "time since the start of the infusion"]], "check": {"t": "One half-life of infusion reaches half of the plateau: half of 20 mg/L is 10 mg/L.", "lo": 5, "hi": 15}, "teach": [{"h": "Whole half-lives of infusion", "list": ["1 − e^(−kt) after 1, 2, 3 half-lives: 50%, 75%, 87.5% of Css.", "The same percentages as the IV bolus table, read upward instead of downward.", "Without a loading dose the level starts at zero and approaches Css over 3 to 5 half-lives."]}], "quote": "So 4 hours, our half-life is 4 hours and we've infused for 4 hours, so 1 half-life. So half of that 20. Is 10, right?", "audit": "Asked aloud on 2 Sep (transcript 09-02, saved as SP/read/tx-0902.txt): \"Without breaking out your calculator, what concentration do you expect?\" Her spoken question is rewritten as one stem. 20(1 - e^-0.693) = 9.9985.", "cite": "3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration Prior to Reaching Steady-State\""},
{"id": "x3-a-ld-4h", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "load", "concept": "loading-plus-infusion", "skill": "conctime", "source": "both", "dupOf": "inf-n9", "stem": "An IV bolus loading dose of 288 mg is administered simultaneously with a continuous infusion of the same drug at 50 mg/hr. The drug has a half-life of 4 hours and a volume of distribution of 12 L. What is the plasma concentration 4 hours after the start of therapy?", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 24, "tol": 0.5, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "Cp = ({{frac:DL|VD}})e^(−kt) + ({{frac:R|k·VD}})(1 − e^(−kt))", "why": "Two inputs run at once, so the level is the falling bolus term plus the rising infusion term."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.17325 hr⁻¹, so kt = (0.17325)(4) = 0.693", "why": "Four hours is exactly one half-life, so both exponentials equal one half."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Bolus term = ({{frac:288 mg|12 L}})(0.5) = (24 mg/L)(0.5) = 12 mg/L", "why": "The loading dose over VD is the level at time zero, 24 mg/L; one half-life later half of it is left."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Infusion term = ({{frac:50 mg/hr|2.079 L/hr}})(0.5) = (24.05 mg/L)(0.5) = 12.0 mg/L", "why": "Cl = k × VD = 2.079 L/hr, so the plateau is 24.05 mg/L, and one half-life of infusion reaches half of it."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = 12 + 12 = 24 mg/L", "why": "The two contributions are concentrations of the same drug in the same volume, so they add."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 24 mg/L", "why": "The level is the same 24 mg/L found at 2 hours, because 288 mg is the steady-state amount: the loading dose was chosen well."}], "setup": {"eq": "load-plus-infusion", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "An \"IV bolus loading dose\" given \"simultaneously with a continuous infusion\", so the sum of the bolus decay and the infusion climb. Four hours is one half-life, so each exponential is one half."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["DL", "288 mg", "over VD gives 24 mg/L at time zero"], ["R", "50 mg/hr", "over Cl gives the plateau"], ["t½", "4 hours", "four hours is one half-life"], ["VD", "12 L", "divides the loading dose and sits in Cl"], ["t", "4 hours", "time since the start of therapy"]], "check": {"t": "Four hours is one half-life: half of the 24 mg/L bolus level is left, and the infusion has climbed half way to its 24.05 mg/L plateau, so the sum stays at 24 mg/L.", "lo": 23, "hi": 25}, "teach": [{"h": "A loading dose that matches the rate", "list": ["DL = Css × VD makes the falling bolus term and the rising infusion term add to Css at every time.", "At one half-life each term is half of 24 mg/L: 12 + 12 mg/L.", "At 2, 4 or 6 hours the level stays at 24 mg/L; a larger loading dose starts above it and a smaller one below."]}], "quote": "What do you think that's gonna be before I even finish it? Should be 12, right? It. Because we just said that we expect it to be 24 throughout.", "audit": "The slide prints \"At 4 hours? At 6 hours?\" after the 2-hour question (inf-n9). Spoken on 2 Sep: bolus 12 mg/L and infusion 12 mg/L at 4 hours. Recomputed 24e^-0.693 + 24.050(1 - e^-0.693) = 12.0018 + 12.0232 = 24.025.", "cite": "3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Example 8\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3c", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "load", "concept": "loading-dose-for-css", "skill": "loading", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. What is an appropriate loading dose for this antibiotic? (Part b: R = 15 mg/hr.)", "units": "mg", "answer": 150, "tol": 2, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "DL = {{frac:R|k}}, or DL = Css × VD", "why": "A loading dose puts the steady-state amount into the body at once. Her key divides the recommended rate by k; the target times the volume gives the same amount."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "k = {{frac:0.693|7 hr}} = 0.099 hr⁻¹", "why": "0.693 over a time in hours leaves reciprocal hours."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "DL = {{frac:15 mg/hr|0.099 hr⁻¹}} = 151.52 mg", "why": "mg/hr over hr⁻¹: the hours cancel and mg is left."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Check: DL = (10 mg/L)(0.231)(65 L) = (10 mg/L)(15.015 L) = 150.15 mg", "why": "10 mcg/mL is 10 mg/L, and 23.1% of 65 kg is 15.015 L, taking 1 kg as 1 L. mg/L times L leaves mg."}, {"k": "round", "t": "DL = 150 mg", "why": "Her key prints 151.52 mg → 150 mg, a practical dose; 151.5 mg and 150 mg are both accepted."}], "setup": {"eq": "dl-rk", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "An \"appropriate loading dose\" for an infusion whose rate is known from part b, so DL = {{frac:R|k}}. k comes first, from the 7-hour half-life. DL = Css × VD gives the same dose from the target."}, "asks": "DL", "givens": [["R", "15 mg/hr", "from part b; divided by k"], ["t½", "7 hours", "gives k = 0.099 hr⁻¹"], ["Css", "10 mcg/mL", "10 mg/L, for the Css × VD check"], ["VD", "23.1% of body weight", "15.015 L in the 65-kg patient"], ["age", "35 years old", "not used"]], "check": {"t": "The loading dose is the amount the body holds at the target: 10 mg/L in 15.015 L is 150.15 mg, and R over k gives 151.52 mg, the same to within rounding.", "lo": 140, "hi": 160}, "teach": [{"h": "Loading dose", "list": ["DL = Css × VD: the amount in the body at steady state, given at once.", "DL = {{frac:R|k}} gives the same amount when R was chosen for that Css.", "She rounds a computed dose to a practical one and prints both: 151.52 mg → 150 mg."]}], "quote": "D_L = 15 mg/hr / (0.693/7 hr) = 151.52 mg → 150 mg", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 c): 15/0.099 = 151.52 -> 150 mg. Css x VD = 10 x 15.015 = 150.15 mg. Keyed 150, tolerance 2 accepts 151.5. Parts a and b of this sheet are inf-n16 and inf-n17.", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part c; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"IV Bolus Loading Dose and Continuous IV Infusion\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3e", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "pre", "concept": "cp-before-steady-state", "skill": "conctime", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. If the drug were infused for 4 hours at the rate you determined above, what would be the concentration of drug in the plasma at the end of the infusion? (Part b: R = 15 mg/hr; part d: ClT = 1.486 L/hr.)", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 3.3, "tol": 0.07, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "Cp = ({{frac:R|ClT}})(1 − e^(−kt)), with t = 4 hr", "why": "The infusion runs for 4 hours, well short of 3 to 5 half-lives, so the level at the end is a fraction of the plateau."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "{{frac:R|ClT}} = {{frac:15 mg/hr|1.486 L/hr}} = 10.094 mg/L", "why": "The plateau the infusion is heading for; mg/hr over L/hr leaves mg/L."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "k = {{frac:0.693|7 hr}} = 0.099 hr⁻¹; 1 − e^(−(0.099)(4)) = 1 − e^(−0.396) = 0.3270", "why": "The bracket is the fraction of steady state reached after 4 hours of infusion; kt has no units."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = (10.094 mg/L)(0.3270) = 3.30 mg/L", "why": "The plateau times the fraction reached gives the level when the infusion stops."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 3.3 mg/L", "why": "Her key prints 3.3 mg/L."}], "setup": {"eq": "cp-infusing", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "\"infused for 4 hours\" and the level \"at the end of the infusion\", before steady state, so Cp = ({{frac:R|Cl}})(1 − e^(−kt)) with t = 4 hr. k from the 7-hour half-life."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["R", "15 mg/hr", "the numerator of the plateau"], ["ClT", "1.486 L/hr", "the denominator of the plateau"], ["t½", "7 hours", "gives k for the bracket"], ["t", "4 hours", "the infusion time in the exponent"]], "check": {"t": "Four hours is a little over half of one 7-hour half-life, so the level is under half of the 10.094 mg/L plateau, about a third of it: 3.3 mg/L fits.", "lo": 0, "hi": 5.05}, "teach": [{"h": "A short infusion", "list": ["An infusion stopped before 3 to 5 half-lives ends below Css.", "Level at the end = Css × (1 − e^(−kT)), with T the time infused.", "That end-of-infusion level, not Css, is where any later decay starts."]}], "quote": "Cp = [15 mg/hr / 1.486 L/hr](1 − e^−(0.099)(4)) = 3.3 mg/L", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 e). Recomputed with R = 15, Cl = 1.486485: 10.0909 x 0.326993 = 3.2997.", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part e; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration Prior to Reaching Steady-State\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3f", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "stop", "concept": "post-cessation-decay", "skill": "conctime", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. If the drug were infused for 4 hours at the rate you determined above, what would be the concentration of drug in the plasma 6 hours after the cessation of the infusion? (Part e: 3.3 mg/L at the end of the 4-hour infusion.)", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 1.82, "tol": 0.04, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "Cp = Cend·e^(−kt), with t counted from cessation", "why": "Once the infusion stops only first-order elimination remains, starting from the level at the end of the 4-hour infusion, not from Css."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "k = {{frac:0.693|7 hr}} = 0.099 hr⁻¹", "why": "The decay needs the rate constant."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = (3.3 mg/L)e^(−(0.099)(6)) = (3.3 mg/L)(0.5521) = 1.82 mg/L", "why": "Six hours after cessation is a little under one half-life, so a little over half of 3.3 mg/L remains."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 1.82 mg/L", "why": "Her key prints 1.82 mg/L."}], "setup": {"eq": "cp-after-stop", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "\"6 hours after the cessation\" of a 4-hour infusion, so the decay line Cp = Cpeak e^(−kt) from the end-of-infusion level, 3.3 mg/L, not from Css."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["Cend", "3.3 mg/L", "the level when the infusion stopped"], ["t½", "7 hours", "gives k"], ["t", "6 hours", "time since cessation"], ["infusion time", "4-hour", "already used to find the 3.3 mg/L"]], "check": {"t": "Six hours is a little under one 7-hour half-life, so a little over half of 3.3 mg/L remains: 1.82 mg/L fits.", "lo": 1.65, "hi": 3.3}, "teach": [{"h": "Which level the decay starts from", "list": ["Stopped after 4 hours: the decay starts at the end-of-infusion level, 3.3 mg/L.", "Run to steady state: the decay starts at Css, 10 mg/L (the next part of this sheet).", "Either way: Cp = Cstart × e^(−kt), with t counted from cessation."]}], "quote": "Cp = (3.3 mg/L)e^−(0.099)(6) = 1.82 mg/L", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 f). Recomputed 3.3 e^-0.594 = 1.8220.", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part f; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration after an IV Infusion has Ended\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3g", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "stop", "concept": "post-cessation-decay", "skill": "conctime", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. If the drug were infused long enough to reach steady-state at the rate you determined above, what would be the concentration of drug in the plasma 8 hours after the cessation of the infusion? (Part b: the rate holds the desired 10 mcg/mL.)", "units": "mg/L", "answer": 4.53, "tol": 0.09, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "Cp = Css·e^(−kt), with t counted from cessation", "why": "This infusion ran to steady state, so the decay starts from Css itself."}, {"k": "unit", "t": "Css = 10 mcg/mL = 10 mg/L", "why": "A microgram per millilitre is the same number as a milligram per litre."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Cp = (10 mg/L)e^(−(0.099)(8)) = (10 mg/L)(0.4529) = 4.53 mg/L", "why": "k = {{frac:0.693|7 hr}} = 0.099 hr⁻¹. Eight hours is a little over one half-life, so a little under half remains."}, {"k": "round", "t": "Cp = 4.53 mg/L", "why": "Her key starts from the 10 mg/L target. Starting from the 10.09 mg/L that 15 mg/hr gives exactly, the answer is 4.57 mg/L; both are accepted."}], "setup": {"eq": "cp-after-stop", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "Infused \"long enough to reach steady-state\", then \"8 hours after the cessation\", so the decay line Cp = Cpeak e^(−kt) with Cpeak = Css, 10 mg/L."}, "asks": "Cp", "givens": [["Css", "10 mcg/mL", "the level when the infusion stopped"], ["t½", "7 hours", "gives k"], ["t", "8 hours", "time since cessation"]], "check": {"t": "Eight hours is a little over one 7-hour half-life, so a little under half of 10 mg/L remains: 4.53 mg/L fits.", "lo": 2.5, "hi": 5}, "teach": [{"h": "Which level the decay starts from", "list": ["Run to steady state: the decay starts at Css, 10 mg/L.", "Stopped after 4 hours: the decay starts at the end-of-infusion level, 3.3 mg/L (the part before this one).", "The two stems differ only in how long the drug was infused; read that before choosing the starting level."]}], "quote": "Cp = (10 mg/L)e^−(0.099)(8) = 4.53 mg/L", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 g) starts from the 10 mg/L target: 10 e^-0.792 = 4.5294. With R = 15 mg/hr, Css = 10.0909 and the answer is 4.5706; tolerance 0.09 accepts both.", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part g; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration after an IV Infusion has Ended\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3h", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "css", "concept": "time-to-percent-css", "skill": "apply", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. If the patient suddenly develops partial renal failure, how long would it take for a new steady-state plasma level to be established (assume that 95% of the Css is a reasonable approximation)?", "options": [{"t": "About 4.32 times the new half-life", "correct": true, "why": "95% of Css takes 4.32 half-lives: 0.95 = 1 − e^(−kt) gives t = {{frac:ln 20|k}}. Renal failure lowers clearance and so lengthens the half-life, so the new half-life has to be found first, as her key says."}, {"t": "About 30.3 hours, as before the renal failure", "why": "Picking this keeps the old half-life. 30.3 hours is 4.32 half-lives of 7 hours; with less clearance k is smaller, the half-life is longer, and the new plateau is reached later."}, {"t": "About 4.32 times the original 7-hour half-life", "why": "This counts the right number of half-lives with the wrong half-life. After renal failure k = {{frac:ClT|VD}} falls, so each half-life is longer than 7 hours."}, {"t": "No time: the level stays at the old steady state", "why": "Picking this treats the plateau as fixed once reached. Css = {{frac:R|ClT}}; when ClT falls at the same rate R, the level rises toward a new, higher plateau, and that climb takes 4.32 of the new half-lives."}], "teach": [{"h": "A change in clearance during an infusion", "list": ["Css = {{frac:R|ClT}}: lower clearance at the same rate gives a higher plateau.", "k = {{frac:ClT|VD}}: lower clearance gives a smaller k and a longer half-life.", "The time to the new plateau is set by the new half-life: 3 to 5 half-lives, or 4.32 for 95%."]}], "quote": "One would have to establish the new t½ for the patient, but the time to reach Css would be 4.32 t½ (see part a)", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 h) is prose only; options built from her part a (30.3 hr = 4.32 x 7 hr) and Practice 4 part g (k = ClT/VD falls with renal clearance).", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part h; IV-Infusions-Practice-4-Solutions.pdf, part g; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration at Steady-State\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3i-r", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "rate", "concept": "rate-for-target-css", "skill": "infusion", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. What rate of infusion and loading dose would you recommend to achieve a steady-state concentration of 30 mg/L? (Answer here: the rate of infusion.)", "units": "mg/hr", "answer": 45, "tol": 0.9, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "R = Css × ClT = Css × k × VD", "why": "At steady state the rate in equals the rate out, ClT × Css, so the rate for a target is the target times clearance."}, {"k": "unit", "t": "VD = (0.231)(65 kg) = 15.015 L", "why": "23.1% of body weight, taking 1 kg as 1 L."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "k = {{frac:0.693|7 hr}} = 0.099 hr⁻¹; ClT = (0.099 hr⁻¹)(15.015 L) = 1.486 L/hr", "why": "hr⁻¹ times L gives L/hr, the volume of plasma cleared each hour."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "R = (30 mg/L)(1.486 L/hr) = 44.6 mg/hr", "why": "mg/L times L/hr leaves mg/hr."}, {"k": "round", "t": "R = 44.6 mg/hr → 45 mg/hr", "why": "Her key prints 44.6 mg/hr → 45 mg/hr as the practical rate; both are accepted."}], "setup": {"eq": "css", "pre": ["thalf-first", "cl-k-vd"], "why": "\"rate of infusion\" to \"achieve a steady-state concentration\", so Css = {{frac:R|Cl}} rearranged to R = Css × Cl. Cl = k × VD, with k from the 7-hour half-life and VD from 23.1% of 65 kg."}, "asks": "R", "givens": [["Css", "30 mg/L", "the new target"], ["t½", "7 hours", "gives k = 0.099 hr⁻¹"], ["VD", "23.1% of body weight", "15.015 L for 65 kg"], ["weight", "65 kg", "with 23.1% gives VD"]], "check": {"t": "Clearance is 1.486 L/hr, a little under one and a half L/hr, and 30 mg/L times that is a little under 45 mg/hr: 44.6 mg/hr, rounded to 45 mg/hr.", "lo": 40, "hi": 50}, "teach": [{"h": "A new target", "list": ["R = Css × Cl: the rate is proportional to the target, since Cl does not change.", "Three times the 10 mg/L target needs three times the rate: 14.86 mg/hr becomes 44.6 mg/hr.", "The loading dose scales the same way: DL = Css × VD."]}], "quote": "R = Css·V_D·k = (30 mg/L)(0.231)(65 L)(0.693/7 hr) = 44.6 mg/hr → 45 mg/hr", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 i): 30 x 0.231 x 65 x 0.099 = 44.595 -> 45 mg/hr. Keyed 45, tolerance 0.9 accepts 44.6.", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part i; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"Drug Concentration at Steady-State\""},
{"id": "x3-a-p3i-l", "prof": "Mosley", "tier": "new", "exam": 1, "type": "numeric", "module": 3, "lecture": "L04", "topic": "infusion", "sub": "load", "concept": "loading-dose-for-css", "skill": "loading", "source": "slide", "stem": "A female patient (35 years old, 65 kg) with normal renal function is to be given a drug by IV infusion. According to the literature, the elimination half-life of this drug is 7 hours and the apparent VD is 23.1% of body weight. The pharmacokinetics of this drug assumes a first-order process. The desired steady-state plasma level for this antibiotic is 10 mcg/mL. What rate of infusion and loading dose would you recommend to achieve a steady-state concentration of 30 mg/L? (Answer here: the loading dose.) (Part i: R = 45 mg/hr.)", "units": "mg", "answer": 455, "tol": 6, "steps": [{"k": "setup", "t": "DL = {{frac:R|k}}, or DL = Css × VD", "why": "The loading dose is the amount in the body at the new steady state, given at once."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "DL = {{frac:45 mg/hr|0.099 hr⁻¹}} = 454.5 mg", "why": "k = {{frac:0.693|7 hr}} = 0.099 hr⁻¹; mg/hr over hr⁻¹ leaves mg."}, {"k": "algebra", "t": "Check: DL = (30 mg/L)(15.015 L) = 450.45 mg", "why": "The target times the volume, 23.1% of 65 kg; it differs from 454.5 mg only by the rounding of the rate to 45 mg/hr."}, {"k": "round", "t": "DL = 454.5 mg → 455 mg", "why": "Her key prints 454.5 mg → 455 mg; a dose near 450 mg is also accepted."}], "setup": {"eq": "dl-rk", "pre": ["thalf-first"], "why": "A \"loading dose\" for the infusion at the rate just recommended, so DL = {{frac:R|k}} with k from the 7-hour half-life. DL = Css × VD gives the same dose to within rounding."}, "asks": "DL", "givens": [["R", "45 mg/hr", "from the rate part; divided by k"], ["t½", "7 hours", "gives k = 0.099 hr⁻¹"], ["Css", "30 mg/L", "with VD gives the Css × VD check"], ["VD", "23.1% of body weight", "15.015 L for 65 kg"]], "check": {"t": "30 mg/L in 15.015 L is 450.45 mg, and 45 mg/hr over 0.099 hr⁻¹ gives 454.5 mg; 455 mg fits.", "lo": 440, "hi": 470}, "teach": [{"h": "Loading dose for a new target", "list": ["DL = Css × VD, or {{frac:R|k}} once R is set for that Css.", "Three times the target, three times the loading dose: about 150 mg for 10 mg/L, about 455 mg for 30 mg/L.", "She prints the computed dose and the practical one: 454.5 mg → 455 mg."]}], "quote": "D_L = 45 mg/hr / (0.693/7 hr) = 454.5 mg → 455 mg", "audit": "Her key (STYLE.md M3-3 i): 45/0.099 = 454.55 -> 455 mg. Css x VD = 450.45 mg. Tolerance 6 accepts both.", "cite": "IV-Infusions-Practice-3---Solutions.pdf, part i; 3IntravenousInfusions.pdf, slide \"IV Bolus Loading Dose and Continuous IV Infusion\""}
];
